# The health-signal monitoring key (specs/linux-migration Task 10, REQ-E1.6).
# Managed by roles/linux; deployed to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys.monitoring, which
# sshd reads because of the 61-monitoring.conf drop-in.
#
# Kept OUT of ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on purpose. That file is the login lifeline
# into a headless server and is not repo-managed; a bug in a template here must
# not be able to lock you out. Failure is contained to the monitor.
#
# This is a PUBLIC key, so committing it is safe -- same reasoning as
# roles/ssh/files/allowed_signers. The private half lives only on `work`, the
# runner machine, and is deliberately NOT in 1Password: REQ-F1.2 exempts "the
# dedicated forced-command monitoring key on its runner machine" precisely
# because it has to be usable unattended, with no human to approve a vault
# prompt. Its safety comes from the restriction below, not from where it is
# stored.
#
# WHAT THIS KEY CAN DO, exhaustively: run
# /usr/local/bin/dotfiles-health-report, which prints one line of capacity and
# liveness numbers and exits.
#
#   command="..."  sshd runs exactly this and ignores whatever the client asks
#                  for, so the key cannot be repurposed into a shell even by
#                  someone holding it.
#   restrict       denies everything else in one word -- no pty, no port or
#                  agent or X11 forwarding, no user rc file. Preferred over
#                  spelling out no-port-forwarding,no-agent-forwarding,... because
#                  `restrict` is deny-by-default: options added by future OpenSSH
#                  versions are denied automatically rather than silently allowed
#                  until someone remembers to add them here.
#
# Rotating: generate a new pair on `work`
# (`ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/id_monitoring -N ''`), replace the line
# below, and re-run the playbook. Nothing else references it.
command="/usr/local/bin/dotfiles-health-report",restrict ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIL1bPWwqLwAAHLj1FLdGhZqMzF1PPM4xDX1wog05xk2M dotfiles health monitor
